[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last major question before I present the design. This one's about the surface.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a notification is transient or durable. That determines whether you need stored read state, which is
  schema, not styling.

  - [1mBell + inbox panel (durable):[0m a badge count and a dropdown list of unread items, each linking to its task; marks
    read on open or click. Requires per-user read state in the store. This is the shape that survives into the server
    version, because "what have I not seen since I was last here" only means something if it's persisted.
  - [1mToasts only (transient):[0m a corner popup on change, gone in a few seconds. No read state, no storage, least code. But
    with a user switcher, a notification fires while the other user isn't watching — so a transient-only design drops
    the very notifications this app is built to deliver.
  - [1mBoth:[0m toast for the live event, inbox for the record. Modest extra work over the inbox alone, since the toast reads
    the same event.
  - [1mBrowser [38;5;153mNotification[39m API (OS-level):[0m desktop notifications outside the tab. Needs a permission prompt, degrades when
    denied, and is genuinely useful only once a server can push — premature here.

  [1mMy recommendation: bell + inbox, with toasts deferred.[0m The inbox is load-bearing and the toast is decoration on top of
  it; building the inbox first means the toast is later a few lines against an event you already emit. Skipping the
  inbox to get toasts is the reverse order and rules out the actual requirement.
